 leans Republican by about eight points, facing Democrat Jennifer Jenkins, a former school board member who switched targets after redistricting. Haridopolos holds a clear financial edge, with about $1.2 million on hand to Jenkins' $108,000, and has drawn endorsements including the U.S. Chamber of Commerce. Jenkins has raised a respectable $726,000 but enters the fall at a cash disadvantage.\n\nRaters overwhelmingly see the seat as safe or solid Republican, with only a few calling it merely likely R. The limited polling, conducted in July by partisan sponsors, shows Haridopolos ahead by roughly nine points, and prediction markets price his win probability around 95%. Jenkins' path likely depends on raising her profile and narrowing the race among low-information voters, but the evidence points to a comfortable Republican hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0724, "margin": -8.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0723, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0928, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0611, "margin": -10.0}]}